// Instants and timestamps -- `Instant`, a reading of one of two clocks, and the
// four things a caller may do with readings: build one, subtract two of the
// same clock, add a `Duration`, compare two of the same clock (cycle 0.2.0;
// `TIME_MODEL.md` M-2 and M-4 and `HOST.md` H-6, as TM-215 and TM-216 amend
// them); `Timestamp`, a point on the absolute UTC scale, with its one
// constructor, which refuses a `secs` outside the range and a `nanos` outside
// one second (cycle 0.2.1; `TIME_MODEL.md` §3 and M-6 ... M-8, TM-219 and
// TM-220); and the two conversions between that scale and the civil one,
// `timestamp_to_utc` and `civil_to_utc` (cycle 0.2.2; `TIME_MODEL.md` M-11 and
// §9, TM-222); and the `Duration` interop -- the four constructors `ntime` adds
// to the prelude's, `timestamp_add` and `timestamp_since` (cycle 0.2.3;
// `SPAN_MODEL.md` N-2 and §5, `TIME_MODEL.md` M-18 and M-20, TM-233 ...
// TM-236). The clocks themselves are `host`'s, since cycle 0.3.0 (TM-248):
// `host_now_instant` and `host_now_boot` build their answers through
// `instant_of`, and `host_now_utc` through `timestamp_of`, which is why both
// are public -- "cycle 0.3's", and "will build", until then. (Until
// cycle 0.2.2 this said the conversions were that cycle's, and until cycle
// 0.2.3 that the interop was.)
//
// A TIMEOUT CANNOT BE WRITTEN AGAINST A WALL CLOCK BY ACCIDENT, and this
// module is half of why: this library offers no conversion between an
// `Instant` and anything else (TM-010), and a consumer can neither write an
// `Instant` as a literal nor edit one -- both fields are `sealed`, so
// `Instant{ ns: n, clock: c }` and `a.ns = n` are `NITPICK-TYPE-079` outside
// this module (`tests/probe/probe20_instant_literal_refused.npk`; the missing
// conversion is `probe20b`). What a consumer CAN write is a construction:
// `instant_of` takes any `int64`, so a number computed from a `Timestamp`'s
// fields becomes an `Instant` that names, in writing, the clock it claims to
// have read -- TM-216's opt-out in writing, which `probe20g` compiles and
// runs, so this paragraph is checked (TM-243; `meta/OPEN_QUESTIONS.md` Q-7
// asked the author whether the constructor's name should say so, and the
// answer, A, is that it keeps its name: TM-248). (Until cycle
// 0.2.4b this said a consumer could not build an `Instant` from a number --
// the cycle audit's C2.) The compiler's runtime says the same of its own
// deadline clock -- `runtime/npkrt.ll` at the pin, the block headed "the
// monotonic clock (D-176, 1.1.3)", above `npk_chain_reset`:
//
//     CLOCK_MONOTONIC nanoseconds since an arbitrary epoch -- the deadline
//     substrate's one clock. Wall clocks are excluded from the deadline path:
//     NTP steps them, and a deadline that moves with the wall silently voids
//     D-056's containment.
//
// AND THE REFUSAL IS THE TYPE'S, IN BOTH DIRECTIONS (TM-221). An `Instant`
// handed where a `Timestamp` is taken, and a `Timestamp` where an `Instant` is,
// are each `NITPICK-TYPE-007` (`probe20c`, `probe20d`); and the language's own
// unchecked cast refuses both directions, `NITPICK-TYPE-032` -- "these types
// have no values in common. `=>!` does not help" (`probe20e`, `probe20f`) --
// though each type is 16 bytes. What remains is `wild` storage reinterpreted
// through a pointer cast, the opt-out `CALENDAR.md` C-8c names for every
// checked property -- and the construction from numbers above, which is
// written, not converted.
//
// TWO CLOCKS, ONE TYPE, AND THE TAG IS AN ENUM (TM-215). `Boottime` counts
// suspend and `Monotonic` does not (`HOST.md` H-6), so a reading of one less a
// reading of the other is a number that means nothing: `instant_since` and
// `instant_cmp` refuse such a pair with `cal`'s `ETimeValue` --
// `ValueFault.ClockMismatch` -- and `instant_add` keeps the clock it was given
// (TM-216). The tag is an enum and not H-6's `uint8`, which holds 254 values
// that are no clock (`SAFETY.md` S-15c). Neither type derives `Ord`: a derived
// order would compare two clocks' readings as one. Both derive `Copy` -- an
// `int64` and a payload-free enum own nothing -- so a consumer's
// `Vec<Instant>` holds them and reads them back (`Vec<T: Copy>` since cycle
// 0.2.0b). The clock is read as the field, `i.clock`: a sealed field is read
// anywhere and written only here, which is the read-only exposure O-X3 asked
// for, and there is no accessor function beside it. `ns` is sealed and NOT
// `hidden` -- the author's answer to the workbench's question 15, recorded in
// TM-219: a consumer can read two clocks' numbers and subtract them by hand,
// which only `instant_since` and `instant_cmp` refuse, and hiding `ns` would
// not close that, because the floor's `mono_now()` hands any program the same
// raw number; a readable reading serves logging.
//
// ONE REPRESENTATION PER INSTANT (TM-219, TM-220). A `Timestamp` is seconds
// since 1970-01-01T00:00:00Z and a non-negative remainder of nanoseconds below
// one second (M-7) -- so half a second before the epoch is `secs: -1, nanos:
// 500_000_000`, and every instant has exactly one spelling. THE FIELD ORDER IS
// THE COMPARISON (M-6, TM-011): `Ord` is derived, so the seconds decide and the
// nanoseconds break a tie -- `tests/probe/probe01_derive_ord.npk` measured the
// derive on this shape at cycle 0.0.0, and `tests/unit/timestamp_order.npk`
// asserts it on the type itself, negative seconds included. Both fields are
// `sealed`, by `Instant`'s reason: a consumer reads `t.secs` and `t.nanos` and
// builds a `Timestamp` only through `timestamp_of`, so neither a literal nor a
// write can spell a denormalised one (`probe21`, `probe21b`). `Copy` is
// derived, so a `Vec<Timestamp>` holds them.
//
// `timestamp_of` VALIDATES AND NEVER NORMALISES. A `secs` outside the supported
// range is `ETimeValue`, `ValueFault.YearRange` (M-8, `HOST.md` H-8), and a
// `nanos` outside `[0, NTIME_NANOS_PER_SEC)` is `ValueFault.NanoRange`, each
// checked before the one narrowing (`SAFETY.md` S-15b): a pair that would need
// carrying is a second spelling of another instant, and it is refused at the
// door rather than repaired. It takes `int64`s, as `civil_time` does, because
// anything narrower pushes the range to the caller -- and a `timespec`'s
// nanoseconds are an `int64` (H-4).
//
// THE CONVERSIONS ARE `cal`'S ALGORITHMS, AND EVERY DAY IS 86 400 SECONDS
// (M-11, TM-222). `timestamp_to_utc` splits the seconds into a day number and
// a second of the day by a FLOOR division, and hands the first to
// `days_to_date` and the second to `civil_time`. `/` truncates toward zero here
// (`tests/probe/probe07_negative_div.npk`), so a negative remainder moves the
// day back by one: `-1 s, 500 000 000 ns` is 1969-12-31T23:59:59.5, the last
// half-second of day -1, and a truncating split hands `civil_time` a second of
// -1 instead. Neither callee can refuse what a `Timestamp` from `timestamp_of`
// hands it -- its range IS the civil range in seconds (M-8), and its remainder
// lies below one second (M-7) -- so the function is `never fails`, as
// `TIME_MODEL.md` §9 says, and each refusal ends in `#unreachable()`:
// `SAFETY.md` S-15c's answer for a value whose range holds by construction. A
// `Timestamp` outside the range can only come from `wild` storage
// reinterpreted, and it stops there, through the floor's `Unreachable` -- an
// arm every consumer owes already. (The statement
// form, `if (r.is_error) { #unreachable(); }`, leaves `r.value` tainted --
// `NITPICK-TAINT-001`, measured at planning: the taint analysis does not count
// the builtin as leaving the block. `?|`'s fallback is the builtin itself,
// which the compiler types as whatever its position expects.) (Cycle 0.2.4b:
// that refusal is a compiler defect and not the language's rule -- the
// workbench registry's O-N34, restated in `meta/OPEN_QUESTIONS.md`, the cycle
// audit's S2: the compiler's own decisions say an arm that ends in the builtin
// leaves. At this pin it stands, so `?|` is the spelling, and the adoption that
// moves the pin re-measures it (TM-245).)
//
// `civil_to_utc` IS FALLIBLE, AS §9 SAYS, AND THAT IS WHAT MAKES IT TOTAL. It
// adds the day number `date_to_days` gives to the second of the day and builds
// its answer through `timestamp_of`, the one constructor, so the range check
// and M-7 are that function's, and nothing here writes a `Timestamp` literal.
// A `CivilDateTime` built from the constructors always converts -- its date
// lies in the range -- so the refusal is reached only through C-8c's opt-out:
// a forged year of 10 000, or a forged `nanos` of one second, is refused, and
// never trapped or carried (`tests/unit/civil_to_utc_edges.npk`, which is
// `VERIFICATION.md` P-4's stand-in for this function). A forged reading whose
// sum `timestamp_of` accepts is answered as the arithmetic gives it: an hour of
// 24, which C-9 refuses and the seal keeps out, is the next day's midnight,
// measured at cycle 0.2.4b's planning -- a misleading answer and no
// misbehaviour, which C-8b allows (the cycle audit's K2). That is `CALENDAR.md`
// C-8b's second bullet -- total where it can be. And nothing here can trap on
// the way: a day number from any `int32` year is at most 7.84e11 in magnitude
// (C-12), times 86 400 is 6.8e16, and the second of the day is at most 933 555
// from three `uint8`s, all far inside `int64`.
//
// THE GATE is the round trip, both ways, each direction handed a walk's value
// rather than the value under test (`TESTING.md` V-4b): both sides of every
// day boundary in the range, `tests/unit/sweep/every_day_boundary.npk`, and
// every second of 512 days chosen by a seeded generator,
// `tests/unit/sweep/every_sampled_second.npk`.
//
// `raw` BEFORE EVERY `never fails` CALL FROM A FALLIBLE BODY: `duration_ns` is
// the prelude's, `never fails`, and `pass duration_ns(...)` inside
// `instant_since` is `NITPICK-TYPE-007` without it (measured at planning).
// `instant_add` overflows only 292 years past the clock's origin, and traps
// there (D-210), as `TIME_MODEL.md` §9's row has said since cycle 0.0 -- `never
// fails`, the trap its range check (it said §8 until cycle 0.2.4b, and §8's
// table has no `Instant` row: the cycle audit's S1). `instant_since` does not
// trap: since cycle 0.2.4b it computes the difference in `int128` and refuses
// one past `Duration`'s range, `ETimeValue`/`Overflow` (TM-242). M-4's "cannot
// overflow in practice" is no premise for a library whose `instant_of` takes
// any `int64`, and a pair it built trapped `IntOverflow` until then -- the
// cycle audit's C6.
//
// FOUR CONSTRUCTORS, AND NO TYPE (TM-004, N-2; TM-233). `duration_mins`,
// `duration_hours`, `duration_days` and `duration_weeks` multiply in `int64` and
// hand the seconds to the prelude's `duration_secs`, whose own multiplication by
// 10^9 traps past `Duration`'s ±292 years -- D-210's `IntOverflow`, which is the
// constructors' range check, as it is the prelude's; so each is `never fails`.
// A day is exactly 86 400 x 10^9 nanoseconds, `NTIME_SECS_PER_DAY` read by name,
// and NOT a calendar day: in a zone with DST a day is 23 or 25 hours, and
// `Period{ days: 1 }` is the thing that means the same wall time tomorrow
// (`SPAN_MODEL.md` N-2's note, §3). A week is seven of them.
//
// `timestamp_add` RE-ESTABLISHES M-7 RATHER THAN REFUSING IT (TM-234). It
// splits the `Duration` by `/` and `%`, which truncate, so the remainder keeps
// `d`'s sign; added to `t.nanos`, the nanoseconds lie in (-10^9, 2 x 10^9), and
// one borrow or one carry puts them back in [0, 10^9). The answer is built by
// `timestamp_of`, the one constructor, so the range check is its own, and a sum
// outside the range is `ETimeValue` -- `ValueFault.YearRange`, as every `secs`
// outside the range is. AND IT CHECKS ITS OPERAND FIRST (`SAFETY.md` S-12,
// TM-241): a `t.secs` outside the range is refused before the arithmetic, with
// `timestamp_of`'s own answer for that `secs`, so the seconds stay within 3.9 x
// 10^11 of the epoch through the add, the borrow and the carry -- a `Duration`
// moves them by at most 9.3 x 10^9 -- and nothing here can trap, whatever `t`
// holds. A `Timestamp` forged through the opt-out is answered with a
// `Timestamp` or refused (`tests/unit/timestamp_add_edges.npk`,
// `VERIFICATION.md` P-4's stand-in for this function, TM-235). (Until cycle
// 0.2.4b the check was the sum's alone, and a forged `secs` within 292 years of
// either end of `int64` trapped `IntOverflow` before `timestamp_of` ran -- the
// cycle audit's C1.)
//
// `timestamp_since` COMPUTES IN `int128` AND NARROWS ONCE (TM-236; M-18, M-20).
// Two readings' seconds differ by as much as the range is wide, about 6.3 x
// 10^11, and times 10^9 that passes `int64` beyond ±292 years, `Duration`'s
// range; so the difference is computed in `int128` -- which holds the
// difference of any two `int64`s times 10^9, so even a forged pair cannot
// overflow it -- checked against `NTIME_DURATION_NS_MIN` and
// `NTIME_DURATION_NS_MAX`, and refused past them, `ETimeValue`/`Overflow`: it
// does not trap, saturate or lose precision.
// `SPAN_MODEL.md` §5 marks it an `int128` site, as it marks `instant_since`
// since cycle 0.2.4b, and `check_int128_sites` holds both to that. The
// difference is nominal -- the scale's, not the SI time that
// elapsed between the readings, which leap seconds make different (M-12). The
// calendar-scale answer, `timestamp_until`, is cycle 0.7.3's, beside
// `date_until` (TM-237).
//
// WHAT IT MAY IMPORT: `cal` and `core` (`BUILD.md` B-17). It imports `cal` for
// `ETimeValue` and, since cycle 0.2.2, for the three civil types and the three
// functions the conversions are made of; and `core`'s `limits` for the three
// bounds `timestamp_of` checks, since 0.2.2 the seconds in a day, and since
// 0.2.3 `Duration`'s two ends -- each number by name, because
// `src/core/limits.npk` holds the one copy of it and `check_constants_named`
// refuses a second in any spelling (`SAFETY.md` S-16, TM-223, TM-231, TM-232).
// (It imported `cal` alone until cycle 0.2.1, and `cal`'s identity and nothing
// else of it until 0.2.2.)
//
// WHAT IT MUST NOT DECLARE: a `Duration` (TM-004). The prelude's is the
// ecosystem's one span type; a second would immediately become the type
// everybody converts to and from. Nor an `error:` -- it raises `cal`'s, and a
// second `ETimeValue` declared here would be a second identity in every
// consumer, which `check_error_budget` refuses (TM-203).
mod:span;

use "../cal/cal.npk".ETimeValue;
use "../cal/cal.npk".CivilDate;
use "../cal/cal.npk".CivilTime;
use "../cal/cal.npk".CivilDateTime;
use "../cal/cal.npk".days_to_date;
use "../cal/cal.npk".date_to_days;
use "../cal/cal.npk".civil_time;
use "../core/limits.npk".NTIME_SECS_MIN;
use "../core/limits.npk".NTIME_SECS_MAX;
use "../core/limits.npk".NTIME_NANOS_PER_SEC;
use "../core/limits.npk".NTIME_SECS_PER_DAY;
use "../core/limits.npk".NTIME_DURATION_NS_MAX;
use "../core/limits.npk".NTIME_DURATION_NS_MIN;

#[derive(Eq, Clone, Debug, Copy)]
pub enum:InstantClock = { Monotonic; Boottime; };

#[derive(Eq, Clone, Debug, Copy)]
pub struct:Instant = { sealed int64:ns; sealed InstantClock:clock; };

// The one constructor, and it checks nothing: a reading's range is its type's,
// any `int64` on either clock, so `VERIFICATION.md` P-3 asks no contract of
// it. A number computed from anything -- a `Timestamp`'s fields included --
// becomes a reading that names, in writing, the clock it claims to have read
// (TM-216; `meta/OPEN_QUESTIONS.md` Q-7 asked whether the name should say
// so, and the author's answer is that it keeps its name: TM-248).
pub func:instant_of = Instant(int64:ns, InstantClock:clock) never fails {
    pass Instant{ ns: ns, clock: clock };
};

// The span between two readings of one clock. Computed in `int128` (M-20;
// `SPAN_MODEL.md` §5 marks the row), refused past `Duration`'s range rather
// than trapped (`SAFETY.md` S-12), and narrowed once.
pub func:instant_since = Duration(Instant:later, Instant:earlier) {
    if (later.clock != earlier.clock) { fail ETimeValue; }   // ValueFault.ClockMismatch
    int128:ns = (later.ns => int128) - (earlier.ns => int128);
    if (ns > (NTIME_DURATION_NS_MAX => int128)) { fail ETimeValue; }   // ValueFault.Overflow
    if (ns < (NTIME_DURATION_NS_MIN => int128)) { fail ETimeValue; }   // ValueFault.Overflow
    // `prove(ns >= (NTIME_DURATION_NS_MIN => int128)
    //        && ns <= (NTIME_DURATION_NS_MAX => int128));` -- P-5
    pass raw duration_ns(ns =>! int64);
};

pub func:instant_add = Instant(Instant:t, Duration:d) never fails {
    pass Instant{ ns: t.ns + d.ns, clock: t.clock };
};

pub func:instant_cmp = Ordering(Instant:a, Instant:b) {
    if (a.clock != b.clock) { fail ETimeValue; }              // ValueFault.ClockMismatch
    if (a.ns < b.ns) { pass Ordering.Less; }
    if (a.ns > b.ns) { pass Ordering.Greater; }
    pass Ordering.Equal;
};

#[derive(Eq, Ord, Clone, Debug, Copy)]
pub struct:Timestamp = { sealed int64:secs; sealed uint32:nanos; };

// `ensures result.secs == secs && (result.nanos => int64) == nanos` --
// `VERIFICATION.md` P-3; and P-4, the invariant to prove first, is the `prove`
// before the narrowing. Comments until the verified build (P-1b), and stood in
// for by `tests/unit/timestamp_construct.npk`.
pub func:timestamp_of = Timestamp(int64:secs, int64:nanos) {
    if (secs < NTIME_SECS_MIN)        { fail ETimeValue; }   // ValueFault.YearRange
    if (secs > NTIME_SECS_MAX)        { fail ETimeValue; }   // ValueFault.YearRange
    if (nanos < 0i64)                 { fail ETimeValue; }   // ValueFault.NanoRange
    if (nanos >= NTIME_NANOS_PER_SEC) { fail ETimeValue; }   // ValueFault.NanoRange
    // `prove(nanos >= 0i64 && nanos < NTIME_NANOS_PER_SEC);` -- P-4
    pass Timestamp{ secs: secs, nanos: nanos =>! uint32 };
};

// The civil reading, in UTC, of a point on the absolute scale (M-11, §9).
// `never fails`: a `Timestamp` is in the range and normalised by construction,
// so neither refusal below can happen to one, and each is S-15c's stop.
pub func:timestamp_to_utc = CivilDateTime(Timestamp:t) never fails {
    int64:days = t.secs / NTIME_SECS_PER_DAY;
    int64:sod = t.secs % NTIME_SECS_PER_DAY;
    if (sod < 0i64) {                                // `/` truncates: floor it
        days = days - 1i64;
        sod = sod + NTIME_SECS_PER_DAY;
    }
    CivilDate:d = days_to_date(days) ?| #unreachable();             // M-8
    CivilTime:c = civil_time(sod / 3600i64, (sod / 60i64) % 60i64, sod % 60i64,
                             t.nanos => int64) ?| #unreachable();   // M-7
    pass CivilDateTime{ date: d, time: c };
};

// The point on the absolute scale a civil reading names, read as UTC (M-11,
// §9). Fallible, and only through `timestamp_of`: a reading from the
// constructors always converts, and a forged one is answered or refused --
// never trapped ("refused, not trapped" until cycle 0.2.4b: the audit's K2).
// `ensures result.secs >= NTIME_SECS_MIN && result.secs <= NTIME_SECS_MAX`
// `ensures (result.nanos => int64) == (c.time.nanos => int64)` --
// `VERIFICATION.md` P-3, a `Timestamp` constructor's; comments until
// the verified build (P-1b), and stood in for by
// `tests/unit/civil_to_utc_edges.npk`.
pub func:civil_to_utc = Timestamp(CivilDateTime:c) {
    int64:days = raw date_to_days(c.date);
    int64:sod = ((c.time.hour => int64) * 3600i64)
                + ((c.time.minute => int64) * 60i64) + (c.time.second => int64);
    pass (relay timestamp_of((days * NTIME_SECS_PER_DAY) + sod,
                             c.time.nanos => int64));
};

// `ntime`'s four constructors (`SPAN_MODEL.md` N-2): each multiplies in `int64`
// and hands the seconds to the prelude's `duration_secs`, whose own `* 10^9`
// traps past `Duration`'s range -- the range check, so each is `never fails`.
// A day is exactly 86 400 x 10^9 nanoseconds, and not a calendar day.
pub func:duration_mins = Duration(int64:m) never fails {
    pass raw duration_secs(m * 60i64);
};

pub func:duration_hours = Duration(int64:h) never fails {
    pass raw duration_secs(h * 3600i64);
};

pub func:duration_days = Duration(int64:d) never fails {
    pass raw duration_secs(d * NTIME_SECS_PER_DAY);
};

pub func:duration_weeks = Duration(int64:w) never fails {
    pass raw duration_secs(w * 7i64 * NTIME_SECS_PER_DAY);
};

// The point `d` from `t` (§9). `t`'s seconds are checked against the range
// first (`SAFETY.md` S-12), so nothing below can trap whatever `t` holds;
// then `/` and `%` truncate, so the remainder keeps `d`'s sign and the
// nanoseconds come out in (-10^9, 2 x 10^9) -- at most 2^32 - 1 + 10^9 for a
// forged `nanos` -- and one borrow or one carry puts a normalised one back in
// [0, 10^9), and `timestamp_of` builds the answer, refusing what one carry
// cannot repair.
// `ensures result.secs >= NTIME_SECS_MIN && result.secs <= NTIME_SECS_MAX`
// `ensures (result.nanos => int64) < NTIME_NANOS_PER_SEC` -- `VERIFICATION.md`
// P-3; comments until the verified build (P-1b), and stood in for by
// `tests/unit/timestamp_add_edges.npk`.
pub func:timestamp_add = Timestamp(Timestamp:t, Duration:d) {
    if (t.secs < NTIME_SECS_MIN) { fail ETimeValue; }    // ValueFault.YearRange
    if (t.secs > NTIME_SECS_MAX) { fail ETimeValue; }    // ValueFault.YearRange
    int64:secs = t.secs + (d.ns / NTIME_NANOS_PER_SEC);
    int64:nanos = (t.nanos => int64) + (d.ns % NTIME_NANOS_PER_SEC);
    if (nanos < 0i64) {                              // `%` kept `d`'s sign: borrow
        secs = secs - 1i64;
        nanos = nanos + NTIME_NANOS_PER_SEC;
    }
    if (nanos >= NTIME_NANOS_PER_SEC) {              // past a second: carry
        secs = secs + 1i64;
        nanos = nanos - NTIME_NANOS_PER_SEC;
    }
    pass (relay timestamp_of(secs, nanos));          // ValueFault.YearRange outside
};

// The nominal span from `earlier` to `later`, in nanoseconds (M-18). Computed
// in `int128` (M-20; `SPAN_MODEL.md` §5 marks the row), checked against
// `Duration`'s range, and narrowed once, after P-5's `prove`; a comment until
// the verified build (P-1b).
pub func:timestamp_since = Duration(Timestamp:later, Timestamp:earlier) {
    int128:ns = (((later.secs => int128) - (earlier.secs => int128))
                 * (NTIME_NANOS_PER_SEC => int128))
                + ((later.nanos => int128) - (earlier.nanos => int128));
    if (ns > (NTIME_DURATION_NS_MAX => int128)) { fail ETimeValue; }   // Overflow
    if (ns < (NTIME_DURATION_NS_MIN => int128)) { fail ETimeValue; }   // Overflow
    // `prove(ns >= (NTIME_DURATION_NS_MIN => int128)
    //        && ns <= (NTIME_DURATION_NS_MAX => int128));` -- P-5
    pass raw duration_ns(ns =>! int64);
};
